WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.781
A robot's past experience can support many future
questions.

2
00:00:03.781 --> 00:00:06.371
This is the promise of spatial memory.

3
00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.845
Where is an object?

4
00:00:08.845 --> 00:00:10.589
Which one do we mean?

5
00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:11.985
What happened, and when?

6
00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:15.124
Each question needs different evidence from the same
experience.

7
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.241
Perception, memory organization, and retrieval offer many
design choices.

8
00:00:22.241 --> 00:00:27.295
A human engineer must combine these pieces into one system
that serves

9
00:00:27.295 --> 00:00:28.980
different kinds of questions.

10
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.117
A coding agent explores these choices through feedback.

11
00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:38.701
It combines modules, evaluates public tasks, and refines
their connections.

12
00:00:38.701 --> 00:00:44.661
The goal is one coherent system, shaped by the full range
of questions.

13
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:47.725
Consider this bedroom.

14
00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:52.402
The memory initially labels a ceiling fan as an aircraft
model.

15
00:00:52.402 --> 00:00:54.953
The designer adds crop based naming.

16
00:00:54.953 --> 00:01:00.904
The retained geometry stays the same, while stored names
and attributes become more useful.

17
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.357
We separate design, build, and query.

18
00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:09.780
After development, implementations are frozen.

19
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.625
They build memories from held out histories without
downstream questions.

20
00:01:14.625 --> 00:01:18.986
The same fixed Query Agent then evaluates each system.

21
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:26.435
Our evaluation brings together public datasets, adapted
annotations, and constructed questions: over

22
00:01:26.435 --> 00:01:32.919
ten thousand queries across five complementary tracks, with
separate public and frozen inputs.

23
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:41.832
Here are the best observed systems from Codex, Claude, and
DeepSeek, alongside established baselines.

24
00:01:41.832 --> 00:01:48.160
Five tracks reveal different strengths: category and
referring expression localization, spatial questions,

25
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.797
temporal questions, and navigation questions.

26
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.741
We combine the five track scores with equal weight.

27
00:01:55.741 --> 00:01:59.764
The best Codex Astra system reaches forty five point seven
five.

28
00:01:59.764 --> 00:02:04.153
Claude and DeepSeek also design competitive systems, ahead
of the full track

29
00:02:04.153 --> 00:02:05.616
baselines in overall score.

30
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.954
We select the agent designed system with the highest five
track Unified

31
00:02:10.954 --> 00:02:13.205
score: Codex with GPT six Astra.

32
00:02:13.205 --> 00:02:17.709
We deploy it in Isaac Sim for navigation and question
answering, testing

33
00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:21.462
whether its memory can support a robot beyond the
benchmark.

34
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:28.365
Now find the sofa closest to the refrigerator.

35
00:02:28.365 --> 00:02:35.216
The agent retrieves candidates, uses their spatial
evidence, and navigates to the selected sofa.

36
00:02:35.216 --> 00:02:40.599
Navigation improves substantially, while whole house
question answering remains a challenge.

37
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:48.805
Coding agents offer a practical way to design spatial
memory around its downstream use.

38
00:02:48.805 --> 00:02:51.529
Spatial memory, by agents, for agents.
